= INTRODUCTION
<intro>
The astronomical evidence for the existence of dark matter, accumulated over decades, is rich and compelling @Zwicky:1933gu@Smith:1936mlg@Rubin1970@Freeman:1970mx@OstrikerPeebles1973@Hoekstra:2003pn@Clowe2006@WMAP2013#cite(<Aghanim:2018eyx>). Yet, the identity and basic properties of dark matter remain shrouded in mystery. An example is the constituent's mass: proposals range from ultra-light $tilde.op 10^(- 22)$ eV @Hu:2000ke to astronomical $tilde.op 10 thin upright("M")_⊙$ @Bird:2016dcv@Garcia-Bellido:2017mdw@Sasaki:2018dmp#cite(<Jedamzik:2020ypm>).

In this vast spectrum, there is nonetheless a useful demarcation point. Dynamical measurements tell us the dark matter mass density in the solar neighborhood is about $0.4 thin "G" e V thin c m^(- 3)$. #footnote[A range of local dark matter density values have been reported in the literature: e.g. $0.008 thin upright("M")_⊙ / upright("pc")^3 = 0.3 thin upright("GeV") / upright("cm")^3$ @Bovy:2012tw, $0.0122 thin upright("M")_⊙ / upright("pc")^3 = 0.46 thin upright("GeV") / upright("cm")^3$ @Sivertsson:2017rkp, $0.013 thin upright("M")_⊙ / upright("pc")^3 = 0.49 thin upright("GeV") / upright("cm")^3$ #cite(<McKee2015>).] From this, one can deduce the average inter-particle separation, #emph[given] a dark matter particle mass. We can compare it against the de Broglie wavelength of the particle:

$ lambda_(upright("dB")) equiv frac(2 pi, m v) = 0.48 thin upright("kpc") (frac(10^(- 22) thin upright("eV"), m)) (frac(250 thin upright("km") / upright("s"), v)) = 1.49 thin upright("km") (frac(10^(- 6) thin upright("eV"), m)) (frac(250 thin upright("km") / upright("s"), v)) thin , $<lambdadB> where $v$ is the velocity dispersion of the galactic halo, and $m$ is the dark matter particle mass, for which two representative values are chosen for illustration. #footnote[In this article, $ℏ$ and $c$ are set to unity. In most cases, restoring $ℏ$ is a matter of replacing $m$ by $m / ℏ$. For instance, the de Broglie wavelength is $lambda_(upright("dB")) = 2 pi ℏ / ( m v ) = h / ( m v )$. The Compton wavelength is $lambda_(upright("Compton")) = 2 pi ℏ / ( m c )$.] It can be shown that the de Broglie wavelength exceeds the inter-particle separation if $m thin lt.eq thin 30$ eV. In other words, in a Milky-Way-like environment, the average number of particles in a de Broglie volume $lambda_(upright("dB"))^3$ is:

$ N_(upright("dB")) tilde.op (frac(34 thin upright("eV"), m))^4 (frac(250 thin upright("km") / upright("s"), v))^3 thin . $ For $m lt.double 30$ eV, the occupancy $N_(upright("dB"))$ is so large that the set of particles is best described by classical waves, much as in electromagnetism, a state with a large number of photons is well described by the classical electric and magnetic fields. #footnote[A more precise statement is that a coherent state of photons has negligible quantum fluctuations if the #emph[average] occupation number is large. See e.g. the classic paper by #cite(<Glauber:1963fi>).] The associated wave phenomena is the subject of this review. We emphasize #emph[classical], for large occupancy implies negligible quantum fluctuations. The question of how the classical description relates to the underlying quantum one is a fascinating subject. We unfortunately do not have the space to explore it here @Sikivie:2009qn@Guth:2014hsa@Dvali:2017ruz@Lentz:2018mpf#cite(<Allali:2020ttz>).

Such a light dark matter particle is necessarily bosonic, for the Pauli exclusion principle precludes multiple occupancies for fermions---this is the essence of the bound by #cite(<Tremaine:1979we>, form: "prose"). For concreteness, we focus on a spin zero (scalar) particle, although much of the wave phenomenology applies to higher spin cases as well @Graham:2015rva@Kolb:2020fwh#cite(<Aoki:2016zgp>). There is a long history of investigations of dark matter as a scalar field @Baldeschi:1983mq@Turner:1983he@Press:1989id@Sin:1992bg@Peebles:2000yy@Goodman:2000tg@Lesgourgues:2002hk@Amendola:2005ad@Chavanis:2011zi@Suarez:2011yf@RindlerDaller:2011kx@Berezhiani:2015bqa@Fan:2016rda#cite(<Alexander:2016glq>). Perhaps the most well motivated example is the Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD) axion @Peccei:1977hh@Kim:1979if@Weinberg:1977ma@Wilczek:1977pj@Shifman:1979if@Zhitnitsky:1980tq@Dine:1981rt@Preskill:1982cy@Abbott:1982af#cite(<Dine:1982ah>). Its possible mass spans a large range---experimental detection has focused on masses around $10^(- 6)$ eV, with newer experiments reaching down to much lower values. For recent reviews, see @Graham:2015ouw@Marsh:2015xka#cite(<Sikivie:2020zpn>). String theory also predicts a large number of axion-like-particles (ALP), one or some of which could be dark matter @Svrcek:2006yi@Arvanitaki:2009fg@Halverson:2017deq#cite(<Bachlechner:2018gew>). At the extreme end of the spectrum is the possibility of an ALP with mass around $10^(- 22) - 10^(- 20)$ eV, with a relic abundance that naturally matches the observed dark matter density (see Section #ref(<motivations>, supplement: none)). More generally, ultra-light dark matter in this mass range is often referred to as fuzzy dark matter (FDM). It was proposed by #cite(<Hu:2000ke>, form: "prose") to address small scale structure issues thought to be associated with conventional cold dark mater (CDM) #cite(<Spergel:1999mh>). This is a large subject we will not discuss in depth, though it will be touched upon in Section #ref(<implications>, supplement: none). It remains unclear whether the small scale structure issues point to novelty in the dark matter sector, or can be resolved by baryonic physics, once the complexities of galaxy formation are properly understood #cite(<Weinberg:2013aya>).

In this article, we take a broad perspective on wave dark matter ($m thin lt.eq thin 30$ eV), and discuss novel features that distinguish it from particle dark matter ($m thin gt.eq thin 30$ eV). The underlying wave dynamics is the same whether the dark matter is ultra-light like fuzzy dark matter, or merely light like the QCD axion. The length scale of the wave phenomena (i.e. the de Broglie wavelength) depends of course on the mass. For the higher masses, the length scales are small, which can be probed by laboratory detection experiments. (The higher masses can have astrophysical consequences too, despite the short de Broglie wavelength, for instance around black holes or in solitons, as we will see.) For the ultra-light end of the spectrum, fuzzy dark matter ($m tilde.op 10^(- 22) - 10^(- 20)$ eV), the length scales are long and there can be striking astrophysical signatures, which we will highlight.#footnote[There is a recent flurry of activities on this front, starting from the paper by #cite(<Schive:2014dra>, form: "prose"): #cite(<Schive:2014hza>, form: "prose")@Veltmaat:2016rxo@Schwabe:2016rze@Hui:2016ltb@Mocz:2017wlg@Nori:2018hud@Levkov:2018kau@Bar-Or:2018pxz@Bar:2018acw@Church:2018sro@Li:2018kyk@Marsh:2018zyw@Schive:2019rrw@Mocz:2019pyf@Lancaster:2019mde@Chan:2020exg#cite(<Hui:2020hbq>). A recent review can be found in #cite(<Niemeyer:2019aqm>).] A mass $m thin < thin 10^(- 22)$ eV is possible, but only if the particle constitutes a small fraction of dark matter, for the simple reason that an excessively large $lambda_(upright("dB"))$ precludes the existence of dark matter dominated dwarf galaxies #cite(<Hu:2000ke>). When the mass approaches the size of the Hubble constant today $m tilde.op 10^(- 33)$ eV, the scalar field is so slowly rolling that it is essentially a form of dark energy #cite(<Hlozek:2014lca>). (The distinction between a slowly rolling scalar field as dark energy, and oscillating scalar field as dark matter, is discussed in Section #ref(<motivations>, supplement: none).)

An outline of the article is as follows. Particle physics motivations for considering wave dark matter are discussed in Section #ref(<motivations>, supplement: none). The bulk of this review is devoted to elucidating the dynamics and phenomenology of wave dark matter, in Section #ref(<dynamics>, supplement: none). The observational/experimental implications and constraints are summarized in Section #ref(<implications>, supplement: none). We conclude in Section #ref(<conclude>, supplement: none) with a discussion of open questions and directions for further research. This article is intended to be pedagogical: we emphasize results that can be understood in an intuitive way, while providing ample references. We devote more space to elucidating the physics than to summarizing the current constraints, which evolve, sometimes rapidly.

