= Particle physics motivations
<motivations>
In this section, we describe the axion---the QCD axion or an axion-like-particle---as a concrete example of wave dark matter: (1) how it is motivated by high energy physics considerations independent of the dark matter problem; (2) how a relic abundance that matches the observed dark matter density can be naturally obtained; (3) how it is weakly interacting and cold. Readers not interested in the details can skip to Section #ref(<dynamics>, supplement: none) without loss of continuity.

We are interested in a scalar field $phi.alt$ that has a small mass $m$. A natural starting point is a massless Goldstone boson, associated with the spontaneous breaking of some symmetry. Non-perturbative quantum effects can generate a small mass---hence, a #emph[pseudo] Goldstone boson---or more generally a potential $V ( phi.alt )$, giving a Lagrangian density of the form: #footnote[ By non-perturbative effects, we mean something that is exponentially suppressed in the $ℏ arrow.r 0$ limit, analogous to how the tunneling amplitude in quantum mechanics is exponentially suppressed $tilde.op e^(- S_(upright("instanton")) / ℏ)$. A moderate value for $S_(upright("instanton")) / ℏ$ could yield a small mass, starting from some high energy scale. See @Marsh:2015xka for examples.] <instanton> $ cal(L) = - 1 / 2 partial_mu phi.alt thin partial^mu phi.alt - V ( phi.alt ) thin . $<phiLag> A concrete realization is the axion, which is a real angular field, in the sense that $phi.alt$ and $phi.alt + 2 pi f$ are identified i.e. $phi.alt / f$ is effectively an angle. The periodicity scale $f$, an energy scale, is often referred to as the axion decay constant.

The classic example is the QCD axion, a particle that couples to the gluon field strength and derives its mass from the presence of this coupling (and confinement). It was introduced to address the strong CP (charge-conjugation parity) problem: that a certain parameter in the standard model, the angle $theta_("QCD")$, is constrained to be less than $10^(- 9)$ from experimental bounds on the neutron electric dipole moment. #footnote[ The $theta_("QCD")$ term in the Lagrangian takes the form $cal(L) tilde.op theta_("QCD") G tilde(G)$ where $G$ and $tilde(G)$ are the gluon field strength and its dual. Such a term is a total derivative, yet must be included in the path integral to account for gluon field configurations of different windings. Such topological considerations tell us $theta_("QCD")$ is an angle. With non-vanishing quark masses, a non-zero angle signals the breaking of CP which is severely constrained by experiments. The idea of the QCD axion is to promote this angle to a dynamical field $theta_("QCD") arrow.r phi.alt / f$, thereby allowing a physical mechanism that relaxes it to zero, as suggested by #cite(<Peccei:1977hh>, form: "prose"). The axion $phi.alt$ is the Goldstone boson associated with the breaking of a certain global symmetry, Peccei-Quinn U(1), as pointed out by #cite(<Weinberg:1977ma>, form: "prose")#cite(<Wilczek:1977pj>).

See #cite(<Dine:2000cj>, form: "prose")@Hook:2018dlk for reviews on axions and alternative solutions to the strong CP problem.] <PecceiQuinnU1> It has certain generic couplings to the standard model, allowing the possibility of experimental detection (see below). More general examples---namely, axion-like-particles which have similar couplings to the standard model but do not contribute to the resolution of the strong CP problem---arise naturally in string theory as the Kaluza-Klein zero modes of higher form fields when the extra dimensions are compactified @Green:1987mn@Svrcek:2006yi@Arvanitaki:2009fg@Dine:2007zp@Halverson:2017deq#cite(<Bachlechner:2018gew>).

