]
For illustration, consider a potential $V ( phi.alt )$ of the following form: $ V ( phi.alt ) = Lambda^4 ( 1 - thin c o s thin [ phi.alt / f ] ) thin . $<Vphi> (The QCD axion potential does not have this precise form, but shares similar qualitative features.) The cosine is consistent with the idea of $phi.alt / f$ being an angle. The additive constant is not important for our considerations, and is chosen merely to make $V$ vanish at the minimum $phi.alt = 0$. The mass of $phi.alt$ can be read off from expanding the cosine around $phi.alt = 0$: $m = Lambda^2 / f$. Typically, $f$ is some high energy scale up to Planck scale, while $Lambda$ is exponentially suppressed compared to that (see footnote @instanton), giving a small $m$. For instance, $f tilde.op 10^17$ GeV and $Lambda tilde.op 100$ eV gives $m tilde.op 10^(- 22)$ eV. The QCD axion potential does not have the exact form above @diCortona:2015ldu, but $m tilde.op Lambda^2 / f$ remains true with $Lambda$ being the QCD scale $tilde.op 100$ MeV. For instance, $f tilde.op 10^13$ GeV gives $m tilde.op 10^(- 6)$ eV for the QCD axion.

What determines the contribution of $phi.alt$ to the energy content of the universe today? Here we outline the misalignment mechanism #cite(<Kolb:1990vq>). Consider the equation of motion for a #emph[homogeneous] $phi.alt$ (following from @phiLag) in an expanding background): $ dot.double(phi.alt) + 3 H dot(phi.alt) + partial_phi.alt V = 0 thin , $<phibareom> where $H$ is the Hubble expansion rate. In the early universe, when $H$ is large, Hubble friction is sufficient to keep $phi.alt$ slowly rolling i.e. balancing the last two terms on the left. Thus $V ( phi.alt )$ plays the role of dark energy. The value of $phi.alt$ is essentially stuck at its primordial value---we assume $phi.alt_(upright("primordial")) / f$, the so called misalignment angle, is order unity. #footnote[An interesting variant of the idea, where the primordial $phi.alt$ has a significant velocity, was proposed by #cite(<Co:2019jts>).] The expansion rate drops as time goes on, until $H$ reaches $tilde.op m$. After that $phi.alt$ rolls towards the minimum of the potential and commences oscillations around it. The expansion of the universe takes energy out of such oscillations, diminishing the oscillation amplitude. Subsequently, $phi.alt$ oscillates close to zero, implying it is a good approximation to treat the potential as: $ V ( phi.alt ) tilde.op 1 / 2 m^2 phi.alt^2 thin . $<m2phi2> The energy density contained in the $phi.alt$ oscillations is $ rho = 1 / 2 dot(phi.alt) zws^2 + 1 / 2 m^2 phi.alt^2 thin . $<rho> It follows from @phibareom that $rho$ redshifts like $a^(- 3)$ where $a$ is the scale factor. The $phi.alt$ oscillations, which can be interpreted as a set of particles, therefore have the redshifting behavior of (non-relativistic) matter, making this a suitable dark matter candidate. Following this cosmological history, it can be shown that the relic density today is @Arvanitaki:2009fg@Marsh:2015xka#cite(<Hui:2016ltb>): $ Omega_(upright("axion")) tilde.op 0.1 (frac(f, 10^17 thin upright("GeV")))^2 (frac(m, 10^(- 22) thin upright("eV")))^(1 / 2) thin $<relic> where $Omega_(upright("axion"))$ is the axion density today as a fraction of the critical density. It is worth emphasizing the relic density is more sensitive to the choice of $f$ than to $m$. The value of $10^17$ GeV, close to but below the Planck scale, is motivated by string theory constructions #cite(<Svrcek:2006yi>). #footnote[See @KM@Davoudiasl:2017jke@Alonso-Alvarez:2018tus for recent explorations of model building.] But a slightly different $f$ would have to be paired with a quite different $m$, if one were to insist on matching the observed dark matter abundance. Nonetheless, this relic abundance computation motivates the consideration of light, even ultra-light, axions.

The reasoning above essentially follows the classic computation of the QCD axion relic density @Preskill:1982cy@Abbott:1982af#cite(<Dine:1982ah>)---the difference is that while $V ( phi.alt )$ is constant here, it is temperature dependent for the QCD axion. Besides the misalignment mechanism, it is also possible axions arise from the decay of topological defects, if the Peccei-Quinn U(1) symmetry is broken after inflation @Gorghetto:2020qws#cite(<Buschmann:2019icd>).

Aside from having the requisite relic abundance, a good dark matter candidate should be cold and weakly interacting. The coldness is implicit in the misalignment mechanism: the axion starts off as a homogeneous scalar field in the early universe, with the homogeneity guaranteed for instance by inflation. (There are inevitable small fluctuations as well, which is discussed in Section #ref(<implications>, supplement: none).) The weakly interacting nature is implied by the large axion decay constant $f$. Possible interactions include:#footnote[We list here only interactions for a pseudo-scalar like the axion. For a scalar, there are other possibilities; see e.g. #cite(<Graham:2015ouw>).] $ cal(L)_(i n t .)^(upright("self")) tilde.op m^2 / f^2 phi.alt^4 quad , quad cal(L)_(i n t .)^(gamma) tilde.op phi.alt / f F^(mu nu) tilde(F)_(mu nu) quad , quad cal(L)_(i n t .)^(Psi) tilde.op frac(partial_mu phi.alt, f) macron(Psi) gamma^mu gamma_5 Psi thin . $<phiInteractions> The first interaction, a self-interaction of $phi.alt$, follows from expanding out the potential $V ( phi.alt )$ to quartic order; it is an attractive interaction for the axion. The second interaction is with the photon, $F$ and $tilde(F)$ being the photon field strength and its dual (there is an analogous interaction with gluon field strength and its dual for the QCD axion). The third interaction is with a fermion $Psi$, which could represent quarks or leptons. The last two interactions are both symmetric under a shift of $phi.alt$ by a constant, as befitting a (pseudo) Goldstone boson. The generic expectation is that all three coupling strengths are of the order shown, but models can be constructed that deviate from it @KM@Kaplan:2015fuy#cite(<Choi:2015fiu>). The important point is that $f$ is expected to be large, keeping these interactions weak, for both the QCD axion and axion-like-particles. For structure formation purpose, these interactions can be largely ignored, though their presence is important for direct detection and in certain extreme astrophysical environments, as we will discuss below.

= Wave dynamics and phenomenology
<dynamics>
The discussion above motivates us to consider a scalar field $phi.alt$ satisfying the Klein Gordon equation: $ - square.stroked phi.alt + m^2 phi.alt = 0 thin , $<phiKG> which follows from @phiLag with the potential approximated by @m2phi2. Much of the following discussion is not specific to axions---it applies to any scalar (or pseudo-scalar) particle whose dominant interaction is gravitational. Occasionally, we will comment on features that are specific to axions, for instance in cases where their self-interaction is important.

Unlike in @phibareom, here we are interested in the possibility of $phi.alt$ having spatial fluctuations. In the non-relativistic regime relevant for structure formation, it is useful to introduce a complex scalar $psi$ ($phi.alt$ is a real scalar): $ phi.alt = 1 / sqrt(2 m) (psi e^(- i m t) + psi^(*) e^(i m t)) thin . $<phipsi> The idea is to factor out the fast time dependence of $phi.alt$---oscillation with frequency $m$---and assume $psi$ is slowly varying i.e. $|| dot.double(psi) || lt.double m || dot(psi) ||$. The Klein-Gordon equation reduces to the Schrödinger equation: $ i thin partial_t psi = - frac(nabla^2, 2 m) psi + m Phi psi thin . $<psiSchr> Several comments are in order. (1) In what sense is the assumption of $partial_t lt.double m$ non-relativistic? From the Schrödinger equation, we see $partial_t tilde.op nabla^2 / m tilde.op k^2 / m$. Thus $partial_t lt.double m$ is equivalent to $k^2 / m lt.double m$ i.e. momentum is small compared to rest mass. (2) We introduce the gravitational potential $Phi$. Recall that $square.stroked = g^(mu nu) nabla_mu nabla_nu$ contains the metric $g^(mu nu)$, thus gravitational interaction of $phi.alt$ is implicit. For many applications, this is the only interaction we need to include. #footnote[Wave dark matter described as such can be thought of as a minimalist version: the primary interaction is gravitational (though as we will see, other interactions expected for an axion could be relevant in some cases). In the literature, there are studies of models where additional interactions play a crucial role e.g. @RindlerDaller:2011kx@bk15@Fan:2016rda@Alexander:2016glq#cite(<Alexander:2019qsh>). Some of the phenomenology described here, such as wave interference, applies to these models as well.] In principle, the metric should account for the cosmic expansion, which we have ignored to simplify the discussion. Cosmic counterparts of the equations presented here can be found in @Hu:2000ke#cite(<Hui:2016ltb>). (3) Despite the appearance of the Schrödinger equation, $psi$ should be thought of as a (complex) classical field. The situation is analogous to the case of electromagnetism: a state with high occupancy is adequately described by the classical electric and magnetic fields. We will on occasion refer to $psi$ as the wavefunction, purely out of habit.

The non-relativistic dynamics of wave dark matter is completely described by @psiSchr, supplemented by the Poisson equation: $ nabla^2 Phi = 4 pi G rho quad , quad rho = m || psi ||^2 thin . $<poisson> The expression for mass density $rho$ can be justified by plugging @phipsi into @rho, taking the non-relativistic limit and averaging over oscillations i.e. $|| psi ||^2$ has the meaning of particle number density. Strictly speaking, the energy density should include gradient energy which is not contained in @rho. The gradient energy contribution to $rho$ is of order $|| nabla psi ||^2 / m$ which is negligible compared to the rest mass contribution $m || psi ||^2$ in the non-relativistic regime.

An alternative, fluid description of this wave system is instructive. This is called the #cite(<Madelung1927>, form: "prose") formulation #cite(<Feynman>). The mass density of the fluid is $rho = m || psi ||^2$ as discussed. The complex $psi$ can be written as $psi = sqrt(rho / m) thin e^(i theta)$. The fluid velocity $arrow(v)$ is related to the phase $theta$ by: $ arrow(v) = 1 / m arrow(nabla) theta = frac(i, 2 m || psi ||^2) ( psi arrow(nabla) psi^(*) - psi^(*) arrow(nabla) psi ) thin . $<vdef> Notice the fluid velocity is a gradient flow, resembling that of a superfluid. (A superfluid can have vortices as topological defects, see Section #ref(<vortices>, supplement: none).)

With this identification of the fluid velocity, what is normally understood as probability conservation in quantum mechanics is now recast as mass conservation: $ partial_t rho + arrow(nabla) dot.op ( rho arrow(v) ) = 0 thin . $<masscons> The Schrödinger equation possesses a U(1) symmetry, the rotation of $psi$ by a phase. In our context, conservation of the associated Noether current expresses particle number conservation, or mass conservation, as appropriate for the $phi.alt$ particles in the non-relativistic regime.

The Schrödinger equation is complex. Thus, besides mass conservation, it implies an additional real equation, the Euler equation: $ partial_t arrow(v) + ( arrow(v) dot.op arrow(nabla) ) thin arrow(v) = - arrow(nabla) Phi + frac(1, 2 m^2) arrow(nabla) (frac(nabla^2 sqrt(rho), sqrt(rho))) . $<euler> Equations @masscons and @euler serve as an alternative, fluid description to the Schrodinger or wave formulation. The last term in @euler is often referred to as the quantum pressure term. It is a bit of a misnomer (which we will perpetuate!), for what we have is a classical system. Also, the term arises from a stress tensor rather than mere pressure: $ Sigma_(i j) = frac(1, 4 m^2) ( rho^(- 1) partial_i rho partial_j rho - partial_i partial_j rho ) = - frac(rho, 4 m^2) partial_i partial_j thin l n thin rho thin , $ i.e. $partial_i ( nabla^2 sqrt(rho) / sqrt(rho) ) / ( 2 m^2 ) = - rho^(- 1) partial_j Sigma_(i j)$.

#footnote[The Euler equation (combined with mass conservation) can be re-expressed as $partial_t ( rho v_i ) + partial_j ( rho v_i v_j + Sigma_(i j) ) = - rho partial_i Phi$. In other words, the standard energy-momentum tensor components are: $T^0 zws_0 = - rho$, $T^0 zws_i = rho v_i$, and $T^j zws_i = rho v_i v_j + Sigma_(i j)$. It can be shown that $T^j zws_i = T_(j i) = ( 4 m )^(- 1) ( partial_i psi partial_j psi^(*) + partial_i psi^(*) partial_j psi - psi^(*) partial_i partial_j psi - psi partial_i partial_j psi^(*) )$. This $T^j zws_i$ can be rewritten in a more familiar looking way by adding a tensor that is identically conserved: $T^j zws_i arrow.r ( 2 m )^(- 1) ( partial_i psi partial_j psi^(*) + partial_i psi^(*) partial_j psi - delta_(i j) [ psi nabla^2 psi^(*) / 2 + psi^(*) nabla^2 psi / 2 + arrow(nabla) psi dot.op arrow(nabla) psi^(*) ] )$. Note the Euler equation in #cite(<Hui:2016ltb>, form: "prose") has a factor of $rho^(- 1)$ missing in front of the divergence of the stress tensor ($sigma_(i j)$ there differs from $Sigma_(i j)$ here by an overall sign).] The stress tensor represents how the fluid description accounts for the underlying wave dynamics. It shows in a clear way how the particle limit is obtained: for large $m$, the Euler equation reduces to that for a pressureless fluid, as is appropriate for particle dark matter.

We are interested in the opposite regime, where this stress tensor, or the wave effects it encodes, plays an important role.

Incidentally, the insight that the #emph[wave] formulation in the large $m$ limit can be used to model #emph[particle] cold dark matter was exploited to good effect by #cite(<Widrow:1993qq>, form: "prose"). The wave description effectively reshuffles information in a phase-space Boltzmann distribution into a position-space wavefunction. It offers a number of insights that might otherwise be obscure @CU2014@Uhlemann:2018gzz#cite(<Garny:2019noq>).

In the rest of this section, we deduce a number of intuitive consequences from this system of equations---Equations @psiSchr and @poisson in the wave description, or Equations @masscons , @euler and @poisson in the fluid description. Implications for observations and experiments are discussed in Section #ref(<implications>, supplement: none).

== Perturbation theory
<PT>
Suppose the density is approximately homogeneous with small fluctuations: $rho = macron(rho) ( 1 + delta )$ where $|| delta || lt.double 1$. We are interested in comparing the two terms---gravity and quantum pressure---on the right hand side of the Euler equation (@euler). Taking the divergence of both, we find: $ - nabla^2 Phi + frac(1, 4 m^2) nabla^4 delta thin , $ where we have expanded out the quantum pressure term in small $delta$. Employing the Poisson equation $nabla^2 Phi = 4 pi G macron(rho) delta$,#footnote[The removal of $macron(rho)$ as a source for the Poisson equation (the so called Jeans swindle) can be justified in the cosmological context by considering perturbation theory around the Friedmann-Robertson-Walker background. Our expression is correct with $nabla$ interpreted as derivative with respect to proper distance. Likewise, $k_J^(- 1)$ given below is proper distance.] we see that the relative importance of gravity versus quantum pressure is delineated by the Jeans scale: $ k_J = ( 16 pi G macron(rho) )^(1 / 4) m^(1 / 2) thin , $<kJeans>

where we have gone to Fourier space and replaced $arrow(nabla) arrow.r i arrow(k)$. This gives $k_J tilde.op 70$/Mpc today for $m tilde.op 10^(- 22)$ eV. On large length scales $k < k_J$, gravity dominates; on small length scales $k > k_J$, quantum pressure wins. The sign difference between the two terms makes clear quantum pressure suppresses fluctuations on small scales. This is the prediction of linear perturbation theory---we will see in Section #ref(<vortices>, supplement: none) that the opposite happens in the nonlinear regime.

This reasoning tells us the linear power spectrum of wave dark matter should match that of particle dark matter (or conventional cold dark matter) at low $k$'s but be suppressed at sufficiently high $k$'s. The precise transition scale differs from $k_J$ given above---a proper computation must include the effect of radiation in the early universe, and account for the full history, from slow-roll to oscillations, outlined in Section #ref(<motivations>, supplement: none). This was carried out by @Hu:2000ke, who gave $ k_(1 / 2) = 4.5 (frac(m, 10^(- 22) thin upright("eV")))^(4 / 9) thin upright("Mpc")^(- 1) thin $<khalf> as the (comoving) scale at which the linear power spectrum is suppressed by a factor of two, and beyond which the power drops precipitously ($tilde.op k^(- 16)$). This is illustrated in the left panel of Figure~#ref(<wavepicture>, supplement: none). For more recent computations, see @Cookmeyer:2019rna@Hlozek:2016lzm#cite(<Hlozek:2014lca>). If the scalar potential $V ( phi.alt )$ is indeed of the form given in @Vphi,

the computation should in principle account for the full shape of $V ( phi.alt )$ rather than approximating it as quadratic, especially if the primordial $phi.alt$ value is comparable to $f$. This was investigated by @Zhang:2017dpp@Arvanitaki:2019rax, who found that the predicted linear power spectrum is largely consistent with earlier work, unless the primordial $phi.alt$ is extremely close to $pi f$ i.e. the top of the potential. #footnote[Computations of the linear power spectrum discussed above assume the fluctuations are adiabatic i.e. $phi.alt$ fluctuations, like fluctuations in photons, baryons and neutrinos, are all inherited from the curvature, or inflaton, fluctuation. The scalar $phi.alt$ can in addition have its own isocurvature fluctuations (see Section #ref(<implications>, supplement: none)).]

The linear perturbative computation described above is phrased in the fluid picture. A fluid perturbation theory computation up to third order in $delta$ and $v$ was carried out in @Li:2018kyk to obtain the one-loop power spectrum. One could also consider perturbation theory in the wave formulation, expanding in small $delta psi equiv psi - macron(psi)$, where $macron(psi)$ is the homogeneous contribution. Wave perturbation theory turns out to break down at higher redshifts compared to fluid perturbation theory #cite(<Li:2018kyk>). #footnote[Wave perturbation theory requires not only the smallness of $( delta psi + delta psi^(*) ) / macron(psi)$ (which equals $delta$), but also the smallness of $( delta psi - delta psi^(*) ) / macron(psi)$ (it is related to the fluid velocity by $arrow(v) = arrow(nabla) ( delta psi - delta psi^(*) ) / ( 2 i m macron(psi) )$). In other words, wave perturbation theory assumes small $delta$ and $m v / k$, while fluid perturbation theory assumes small $delta$ and $v$. In large scale structure, one is typically interested in situations where $m / k gt.double 1$. Thus perturbation theory breaks down sooner in the wave formulation.]

== Soliton/boson star
<solitons>
The Euler equation is useful for intuiting properties of certain nonlinear, bound objects, known as solitons or boson stars @Kaup:1968zz@Ruffini:1969qy@Friedberg:1986tp@Friedberg:1986tq@Seidel:1993zk#cite(<Guzman:2006yc>). We are interested in objects in which quantum pressure balances gravitational attraction i.e. the two terms on the right hand side of @euler cancel each other: $ frac(G M, R) tilde.op frac(1, m^2 R^2) thin , $ where $M$ is the total mass of the object and $R$ is its radius, and we have replaced $nabla tilde.op 1 / R$ and dropped factor of $2$. This implies the size of the soliton/boson star is inversely proportional to its mass:

$  &  & R tilde.op frac(1, G M m^2) tilde.op 100 thin upright("pc") thin frac(10^9 thin upright("M")_⊙, M) (frac(10^(- 22) thin upright("eV"), m))^2\
 &  & quad tilde.op 300 thin upright("km") thin frac(10^(- 10) thin upright("M")_⊙, M) (frac(10^(- 6) thin upright("eV"), m))^2 tilde.op 50 thin upright("km") thin frac(5 thin upright("M")_⊙, M) (frac(10^(- 11) thin upright("eV"), m))^2 thin , $<Rsoliton> where we give a few representative values of $M$ and $m$.#footnote[This rough estimate is about a factor of 4 smaller than the exact relation #cite(<Chavanis:2011zi>). We focus on spherical solitons. Filamentary and pancake analogs are explored in #cite(<Desjacques:2017fmf>, form: "prose")@Alexander:2019qsh@Mocz:2019pyf, and rotating solitons are discussed in #cite(<Hertzberg:2018lmt>, form: "prose").] The example of $m tilde.op 10^(- 22)$ eV corresponds to that of fuzzy dark matter---such a soliton can form in the centers of galaxies @Schive:2014dra#cite(<Schive:2014hza>, supplement: [see Section #ref(<dynamics2>, supplement: none) below]). The example of $m tilde.op 10^(- 6)$ eV corresponds to that of the QCD axion---such an axion star (often called an axion minicluster) could form in the aftermath of Peccei-Quinn symmetry breaking after inflation @Kolb:1993zz@Kolb:1995bu@Fairbairn:2017sil@Eggemeier:2019jsu#cite(<Buschmann:2019icd>). The example of $m tilde.op 10^(- 11)$ eV could be an axion-like-particle---an object like this has been studied as a possible gravitational wave event progenitor @Helfer:2016ljl#cite(<Widdicombe:2018oeo>).

There is an upper limit to the mass of the soliton: $G M / R thin lt.eq thin 1$ to avoid collapse to a black hole. Plugging in the expression for $R$, we deduce the maximum soliton mass (a Chandrasekhar mass of sort): $ M_(upright("max")) tilde.op frac(1, G m) tilde.op 10^12 thin upright("M")_⊙ (frac(10^(- 22) thin upright("eV"), m)) tilde.op 10^(- 4) thin upright("M")_⊙ (frac(10^(- 6) thin upright("eV"), m)) tilde.op 10 thin upright("M")_⊙ (frac(10^(- 11) thin upright("eV"), m)) thin . $<Mmax1> Strictly speaking, as one approaches the maximum mass, one should use the relativistic Klein Gordon description rather than the Schrödinger equation, but the above provides a reasonable estimate @Kaup:1968zz@Ruffini:1969qy#cite(<Friedberg:1986tq>).

Not all gravitationally bound objects are solitons, of course. The argument above accounts for the two terms on the right of the Euler equation (@euler). The velocity terms on the left could also play a role. In other words, a bound object could exist by balancing gravity against virialized motion instead i.e. $v^2 tilde.op G M / R > 1 / ( m^2 R^2 )$. Most galaxies are expected to fall into this category, supported by virialized motion except possibly at the core where a soliton could condense (see Section #ref(<dynamics2>, supplement: none)).

The discussion so far ignores the possibility of self-interaction. For an axion, we expect a $m^2 phi.alt^4 / f^2$ contribution to the Lagrangian (@phiInteractions). It can be shown the relevant quantities to compare are: $v^2$ (virialized motion), $1 / ( m^2 R^2 )$ (quantum pressure) balancing against $G M / R$ (gravity) and $M / ( m^2 f^2 R^3 )$ (attractive self-interaction of the axion). This can be deduced by comparing the gravitational contribution to energy density $rho Phi$ with the self-interaction contribution $m^2 phi.alt^4 / f^2 tilde.op rho^2 / ( m^2 f^2 )$, and using $Phi tilde.op G M / R$ and $rho tilde.op M / R^3$. The attractive self-interaction is destabilizing, going as $1 / R^3$: if it dominates over gravity, there is nothing that would stop $R$ from getting smaller and making the self-interaction even stronger. Demanding that the $M$-$R$ relation in @Rsoliton satisfies $G M / R > M / ( m^2 f^2 R^3 )$ modifies the maximum soliton mass to @Eby:2015hsq@Eby:2015hyx#cite(<Helfer:2016ljl>): $  &  & M_(upright("max")) tilde.op frac(f, G^(1 / 2) m) tilde.op 10^10 thin upright("M")_⊙ (frac(f, 10^17 thin upright("GeV"))) (frac(10^(- 22) thin upright("eV"), m))\
 &  & quad quad quad tilde.op 10^(- 10) thin upright("M")_⊙ (frac(f, 10^13 thin upright("GeV"))) (frac(10^(- 6) thin upright("eV"), m)) tilde.op thin upright("M")_⊙ (frac(f, 10^18 thin upright("GeV"))) (frac(10^(- 11) thin upright("eV"), m)) thin . $<Mmax2>

