== Galactic dynamics and structure---density profile, stellar scattering, dynamical friction, subhalo mass function and interference substructures
<galacticConstraints>
There is a wide variety of methods to constrain wave dark matter from galactic structure or dynamics, especially at the ultra-light end of the spectrum.

#emph[Density profile.] Wave simulations demonstrate that fuzzy dark matter halos generically have a solitonic core, and an NFW-like outer density profile #cite(<Schive:2014hza>). There is a substantial literature on comparing this prediction against observations. Investigations focusing on the inner density profile (i.e. within the purported soliton) of Milky Way dwarf satellites found reasonable agreement with $m tilde.op 10^(- 22) - 10^(- 21)$ eV @Chen:2016unw#cite(<Calabrese:2016hmp>). A $10^9 thin thin upright("M")_⊙$ soliton at the center of the Milky Way was reported by @DeMartino:2018zkx, though there is substantial uncertainty because of the dominance of baryons #cite(<Li:2020qva>). Investigations bearing on how the soliton connects with the outer halo generally found tension with data, for $m thin lt.eq 10^(- 21)$ eV. Taking the soliton-halo relation (@solitonhalorelation) seriously, one expects an inner circular velocity that matches the outer asymptotic value (a reflection of the rough equality of the soliton potential and halo potential; see footnote @solitonhalo), something not seen in observations of disk galaxies #cite(<Bar:2018acw>). Moreover, dynamical measurements of Milky Way dwarf satellites, when used to fit for solitonic cores, predict halo masses that are too large, incompatible with their survival under dynamical friction, giving a bound of $m thin > thin 6 times 10^(- 22)$ eV #cite(<Safarzadeh:2019sre>). It was also pointed out by @Burkert:2020laq that low mass galaxies have a universal core surface density $tilde.op 75 thin thin upright("M")_⊙ / upright("pc")^2$ while spanning a large range in core radius; this conflicts with the soliton scaling of $M prop 1 / R$ (@Rsoliton) implying a surface density $prop 1 / R^3$. On the other hand, @Pozo:2020ukk pointed out that the stellar density profile of dwarfs matches well the mass density profile in fuzzy dark matter simulations.

Overall, it appears the fuzzy dark matter soliton does not in a straightforward way match galaxy cores seen in dynamical data, when viewed in the larger context of the host halo. A number of possible mitigating factors should be kept in mind. The relaxation time for forming a soliton scales as $m^3$ (@trelax), which can get quite long for the higher masses. Some of the galaxies investigated are in dense environments; tidal interactions could perturb them in significant ways that should be taken into account (see Section #ref(<dynamics2>, supplement: none)). Inference of galaxy density profiles from dynamical data is subject to uncertainty from the velocity anisotropy profile @Walker:2009zp@Amorisco:2011hb, or possible non-circular motions #cite(<Oman:2017vkl>). Baryons and central supermassive black holes could affect galaxy density profiles in non-negligible ways. There has been a lot of work in this direction for conventional cold dark matter, with some success and some remaining puzzles e.g. #cite(<Oman:2015xda>). #footnote[See also @Kaplinghat:2019dhn on the self-interacting dark matter model.] These considerations are likely relevant for testing fuzzy dark matter from density profiles @Bar:2019bqz#cite(<Bar:2019pnz>).

#emph[Heating/scattering of stars.] Transient, de Broglie size substructures due to wave interference heat up stars in a galaxy (Section #ref(<dynamics2>, supplement: none)). Such heating of the Milky Way disc was investigated by @Church:2018sro who put a bound $m thin > thin 0.6 times 10^(- 22)$ eV to avoid overheating. Stellar streams from tidally disrupted globular clusters can be heated up in a similar way, leading to thickening. A bound of $m thin > thin 1.5 times 10^(- 22)$ eV was placed by @Amorisco:2018dcn based on this argument. The stellar cluster at the center of the ultra-faint dwarf Eridanus II was used to place constraints on $m$ by #cite(<Marsh:2018zyw>). Solitons in wave simulations are observed to have oscillations #cite(<Veltmaat:2018dfz>). The oscillation time scale would be shorter than the dynamical time scale of the stellar cluster for $m thin gt.eq thin 10^(- 21)$ eV, leading to heating and disruption of the stellar cluster for $m$ up to $10^(- 20)$ eV. #footnote[For $m thin lt.eq thin 10^(- 21)$ eV, the long soliton oscillation time ($tilde.op 1 / ( m v^2 )$) means the impact on the stellar cluster is adiabatic i.e. no heating. For $m thin gt.eq thin 10^(- 20)$, @Marsh:2018zyw derived constraints not from heating by soliton oscillation, but from heating by de Broglie granules.] The observation of soliton oscillations was based on simulations of isolated halos, while Eridanus II is a Milky Way satellite subject to tidal forces. Recently, a simulation including an external tidal field was described in #cite(<Schive:2019rrw>). They showed that tidal disruption of the outer halo surrounding the soliton leads to suppressed heating of a stellar cluster in the soliton.#footnote[It was pointed out by @Schive:2019rrw that the soliton in general undergoes random walks as well as oscillates. Tidal stripping of the outer halo appears to suppress excitations associated with such processes.]

Analytic arguments suggest the same #cite(<Li:2020ryg>).

#emph[Dynamical friction.] The wave nature of dark matter can lead to a suppression of dynamical friction, as explained in Section #ref(<dynamics2>, supplement: none). It was argued by @Hui:2016ltb that a fuzzy dark matter mass of $m tilde.op 10^(- 22)$ eV helps explain the survival of globular clusters against orbital decay in the halo of Fornax @tre76#cite(<oh2000>). See @Lancaster:2019mde for a numerical exploration of this phenomenon, and @Bar-Or:2018pxz on how the suppression of dynamical friction is tempered by diffusion. It is worth noting that within the conventional cold dark matter model, a possible solution to this dynamical friction problem is to invoke core-stalling @Goerdt:2006rw@Read:2006fq@Inoue:2009wd#cite(<Cole:2012ns>). Dynamical data with higher precision, and on more systems, would be very helpful.

#emph[Subhalo mass function.] Fuzzy dark matter, with its suppressed power on small scales, predicts fewer low mass halos compared with conventional cold dark matter. The same is expected to be true for subhalos of a parent galaxy, such as the Milky Way. Several different ways to probe the subhalo mass function have been discussed in the literature. One way is to infer the subhalo mass function from the observed luminosity function of Milky Way satellites, using abundance matching. This was carried out by @Nadler:2020prv who obtained the bound $m thin > thin 2.9 times 10^(- 21)$ eV. Another method is to use stellar streams from tidally disrupted globular clusters or satellites in our galaxy @Johnston:2001wh#cite(<Ibata:2001iv>). Observed perturbations of streams were used to place constraints on the subhalo mass function, which were then turned into constraints on warm dark matter @Banik:2019smi and fuzzy dark matter @Schutz:2020jox, obtaining $m thin > thin 2.1 times 10^(- 21)$ eV. Yet another method is to use flux anomaly in strongly lensed systems to probe subhalos in the lensing galaxies #cite(<Dalal:2001fq>). This was used by @Gilman:2019nap to constrain warm dark matter and @Schutz:2020jox to limit fuzzy dark matter, obtaining $m thin > thin 2.1 times 10^(- 21)$ eV. A natural question for these investigations is to what extent the subhalo mass function for fuzzy dark matter is accurately known. It is typically computed using Press-Schechter type formalism, meaning the effect of fuzzy dark matter enters only through the initial power spectrum (i.e. its suppression on small scales). Dynamical effects due to wave interference could influence the subsequent evolution, and thus the subhalo mass function. It would be useful to quantify it with wave simulations (see discussion at the end of Section #ref(<linearpowerspectrum>, supplement: none)). Moreover, wave interference granules---not virialized subhalos---could by themselves give rise to these signals, such as the scattering of stellar streams #cite(<Dalal:2020mjw>). Their effects should be taken into account.

#emph[Probing interference substructures.] One generic prediction of wave dark matter is the existence of interference substructures in halos. These are de Broglie scale, order unity density fluctuations. The fluctuation can take the density all the way to zero (complete destructive interference i.e. vortices; see Section #ref(<vortices>, supplement: none)). There are different ways to probe these interference substructures. One is through the heating and scattering of stars, already discussed above. The other is through gravitational lensing by the substructures. For instance, a de Broglie size blob in our own galaxy passing over the line of sight to some distant object would cause the apparent position of that object to shift @Weiner:2019zrg@Mondino:2020rkn@Mishra-Sharma:2020ynk#cite(<Hui:2020hbq>). The effect is small---@Mishra-Sharma:2020ynk proposed the correlated shifts of many distant objects could be used to look for small signals. Another context where a gravitational lensing signal can be searched for is cases of strong lensing. The lensing flux anomaly refers to the phenomenon that strongly magnified images of a distant source have flux ratios that are discordant with expectations from a smooth lensing halo @Mao:1997ek@Chiba:2001wk@Metcalf:2001ap@Dalal:2001fq@Hezaveh:2014aoa@Alexander:2019puy#cite(<Dai:2020rio>). For instance, two images close to a critical line (corresponding to a fold caustic) are expected to have the same magnification, barring substructures on scales smaller than the image separation. It has been shown that interference substructures can cause a $tilde.op 10 %$ difference in cases of high magnification $tilde.op 100$ @Chan:2020exg#cite(<Hui:2020hbq>). Since subhalos also give rise to such flux anomaly, to distinguish between fuzzy dark matter and conventional cold dark matter, a measurement of the anomaly as a function of image separation would be helpful. The anomaly power spectrum of fuzzy dark matter would have a feature around the de Broglie scale.

== Probes using compact objects---superradiance, solitons, potential oscillation and stellar cooling
<probesCombojb>
#emph[Superradiance.] Superradiance constraints on the existence of light scalars, or light bosons more generally--- not necessarily dark matter---were summarized in #cite(<Stott:2018opm>). The idea is to use the measured spin of black holes to put limits on scalars which could drain away their angular momentum, if their Compton wavelength roughly matches the horizon size (see Section #ref(<compactobj>, supplement: none)). The boson mass probed this way covers a wide range, from $tilde.op 10^(- 13) - 10^(- 12) thin upright("eV")$ for black holes at tens of solar mass, to $tilde.op 10^(- 18) - 10^(- 21) thin upright("eV")$ for supermassive black holes. It was pointed out by @Davoudiasl:2019nlo that the spin constraint on the M87 supermassive black hole, reported by the Event Horizon Telescope (EHT) collaboration @Akiyama:2019fyp, disfavors ultra-light bosons around $10^(- 21)$ eV. It is worth noting that the EHT constraint comes not from measurement of the famous shadow, but from modeling of the jet coming out of the galactic nucleus.

The existing superradiance constraints were obtained by assuming the superradiance cloud grows from a small initial seed of superradiance-unstable modes (produced by quantum fluctuations for instance). As pointed out by @Ficarra:2018rfu, the existence of additional superradiance-stable modes could significantly modify the long term evolution of the cloud, and therefore the mass and spin of the black hole (see footnote @nonlinearKG). Such stable modes are naturally present if the light boson in question were the dark matter. Dark matter mass and angular momentum accretion onto the black hole inevitably occurs @Clough:2019jpm@Hui:2019aqm#cite(<Bamber:2020bpu>). It would be useful to revisit the superradiance constraints for cases where the light boson is the dark matter. It is also worth noting that enhanced interactions of the axion could lead to relaxation of the superradiance constraints #cite(<Mathur:2020aqv>).

#emph[Boson stars.] Light boson dark matter can be probed astrophysically in a different way, by the boson stars or solitons that could form in the early universe. Using the Chandrasekhar-like maximum mass as a guide (Equations @Mmax1 or @Mmax2), the interesting boson star mass could range from $10^(- 10) thin upright("M")_⊙$ to $10^10 thin upright("M")_⊙$, for dark matter mass from $10^(- 6)$ eV to $10^(- 22)$ eV. Gravitational lensing could be used to detect or constrain a population of such objects @Kolb:1995bu#cite(<Fairbairn:2017sil>). They could also contribute to merger events seen by gravitational wave experiments if they are sufficiently compact @Macedo:2013jja@Palenzuela:2017kcg@Clough:2018exo#cite(<Helfer:2018vtq>). The computation of the early universe production of boson stars, specifically axion stars, was pioneered by #cite(<Kolb:1993zz>, form: "prose"). Termed axion miniclusters, they form due to large fluctuations from the breaking of the Peccei-Quinn symmetry after inflation. The mass function of boson stars subsequently evolves, due to mergers and condensation processes @Fairbairn:2017sil#cite(<Eggemeier:2019jsu>). Further computations to firm up the prediction of the eventual mass distribution of boson stars would be helpful.

#emph[Gravitational potential oscillations.] An oscillating scalar produces an oscillating gravitational potential at frequency $2 m$, as pointed out by #cite(<Khmelnitsky:2013lxt>, form: "prose"). This effect can be searched for in pulsar timing array data, which has a frequency coverage that probes $m tilde.op 10^(- 24) - 10^(- 22)$ eV. The oscillating potential scales as $rho / m^2$ (see Section #ref(<compactobj>, supplement: none)) so the constraints are stronger at smaller $m$'s. A bound of $rho thin < thin 6 thin upright("GeV") / upright("cm")^3$ for $m lt.eq 10^(- 23)$ eV was obtained by @Porayko:2018sfa from the Parkes Pulser Timing Array data. A bound of $rho thin < thin 2 thin upright("GeV") / upright("cm")^3$ for $m tilde.op 10^(- 23)$ eV was obtained by @Kato:2019bqz from the NANOGrav data. These are proofs of concept, since the local dark matter density is already known to be $rho tilde.op 0.4 thin upright("GeV") / upright("cm")^3$ @Bovy:2012tw@Sivertsson:2017rkp#cite(<McKee2015>). As a probe of wave dark matter, this method is interesting because it directly probes the scalar field oscillations at frequency $m$, and has very different systematics from other astrophysical probes. The solar system ephemeris turns out to be an important source of systematic error. Forecasts of future improvements, with the planned Square Kilometre Array, can be found in #cite(<Porayko:2018sfa>). To place meaningful limits on $m tilde.op 10^(- 22)$ eV, it is important to have high cadence in addition to long integration time.

#emph[Stellar axion emission.] To close this sub-section on compact objects, we mention one classic probe: axion bounds from the cooling of stars. Axion couples to photons, gluons and fermions in the standard model (@phiInteractions). The interaction strength is weak, but deep in the interior of stars, there can be enough axion production to affect stellar structure and evolution. (The weak interaction strength also makes it relatively easy for the axion to escape from the star.) This has been applied to the Sun @Schlattl:1998fz, red giants @Raffelt:1987yu, supernova 1987A @Raffelt:1987yt@Ellis:1987pk@Turner:1987by@Mayle:1987as and neutron star mergers #cite(<Dietrich:2019shr>). #footnote[For 1987A, the axion constraint comes from its effect on the neutrino burst duration. For ways to evade such supernova or stellar cooling bounds, see @Bar:2019ifz#cite(<DeRocco:2020xdt>).] There are also experiments built specifically to detect solar axions such as CAST #cite(<Anastassopoulos:2017ftl>). Phrased in terms of the axion decay constant $f$ (larger $f$ means weaker coupling; see @phiInteractions), the strongest constraint from these considerations is about $f thin gt.eq thin 10^9$ GeV. Note that these constraints on the axion assume only its existence, #emph[not] its viability as a dark matter candidate. A comprehensive recent review can be found in #cite(<Raffelt:2006cw>). There are also proposals to detect axion dark matter from the production of photons in strong magnetic fields around neutron stars @Bai:2017feq@Hook:2018iia#cite(<Foster:2020pgt>).

== Photon propagation in axion background
<photonpropagation>
The axion coupling to $arrow(E) dot.op arrow(B)$ (@phiInteractions) affects the propagation of photons in the universe if dark matter is indeed made up of axions. To be concrete, suppose the Lagrangian for the photon consists of $ cal(L) = - 1 / 4 F_(mu nu) F^(mu nu) + 1 / 4 g_gamma phi.alt F_(mu nu) tilde(F)^(mu nu) thin $<Lphoton> where $F_(mu nu)$ is the photon field strength and $tilde(F)^(mu nu) = epsilon.alt^(mu nu alpha beta) F_(alpha beta) / 2$. The coupling constant $g_gamma$ plays the role of $tilde.op 1 / f$ in @phiInteractions.

The modified Maxwell equations, setting $arrow(E)$ and $arrow(B)$ proportional to $e^(- i omega t + i arrow(k) dot.op arrow(x))$, imply a dispersion relation of the form #cite(<Harari:1992ea>): $ omega = || arrow(k) || plus.minus 1 / 2 g_gamma ( partial_t phi.alt + hat(k) dot.op arrow(nabla) phi.alt ) thin , $ for the two circular polarizations ($plus.minus$). This is obtained assuming the WKB limit (i.e. $partial^2 phi.alt thin lt.double thin omega partial phi.alt$), and small $g_gamma$. The fact that the two circular polarizations have different dispersion relations means a linearly polarized photon rotates in polarization as it propagates. One can phrase this in terms of the phase difference between the two circular polarizations: $ Delta S = g_gamma integral d t frac(D phi.alt, D t) thin , $<DeltaS> where $D / D t$ is a total time derivative: $partial_t + hat(k) dot.op arrow(nabla)$ i.e. the phase for the respective polarization is $S = - || arrow(k) || t + arrow(k) dot.op arrow(x) plus.minus Delta S / 2$. There have been several attempts or proposals to search for this birefringence effect in astronomical data, for instance the polarization of radio galaxies @Carroll:1989vb@Harari:1992ea@Nodland:1997cc@Carroll:1997tc and the microwave background @Harari:1992ea@Lue:1998mq@Liu:2016dcg#cite(<Fedderke:2019ajk>). #footnote[See also @Agrawal:2019lkr for a proposal to look for axion strings in the microwave background polarization data.] Recently, @Ivanov:2018byi proposed and searched for a polarization signal that oscillates in time in observations of jets in active galaxies @Caputo:2019tms#cite(<Fedderke:2019ajk>). The frequency $m$ oscillations in $phi.alt$ cause the linear polarization angle to oscillate, which can be searched for in data. A limit of $g_gamma thin lt.eq thin 10^(- 12) thin upright("GeV")^(- 1)$ was obtained for $m tilde.op 5 times 10^(- 23) - 1.2 times 10^(- 21)$ eV. Note that the birefringence signal does not depend on the distance over which the photon travels; it depends only on the values of $phi.alt$ at the source and at the observer. A source in a high dark matter density environment (therefore large $phi.alt$), such as at the center of a galaxy, is therefore a promising target.

The fact that rotation of the linear polarization angle is independent of propagation distance means one could also search for this effect in the laboratory where high precision measurements are possible e.g. @Liu:2018icu@DeRocco:2018jwe@Martynov:2019azm#cite(<Blas:2019qqp>). This brings us naturally to the subject of the next section. We close by mentioning that the same coupling of the axion to photons (@Lphoton) gives rise to a different effect that can be searched for: the conversion of photons into axions in an environment with magnetic fields @Raffelt:1987im#cite(<Mirizzi:2006zy>). This effect does not require the axions to be dark matter.

== Experimental detection of axions
<axionExp>
The experimental detection of axions is a large subject we cannot hope to do justice here. For recent comprehensive reviews, see e.g. @Graham:2015ouw@Irastorza:2018dyq#cite(<Sikivie:2020zpn>). We instead focus on aspects of the detection that have to do with the wave nature of axion dark matter. This sub-section is less about summarizing current constraints, and more about discussing ways to probe or take advantage of the wave dynamics and interference substructures. #footnote[In this sub-section, we pick a few experiments to illustrate how the wave nature of axions is relevant to detection. There is a tremendous diversity in the variety of axion experiments. Some aim to detect dark matter; some probe the existence of an axion regardless of whether it is dark matter. See @Graham:2015ouw@Irastorza:2018dyq#cite(<Sikivie:2020zpn>).] There are a number of papers on this subject. Novel observables for the detection of the axion as a field (or wave) rather than as a particle were discussed by #cite(<CASPEr>, form: "prose"). Stochastic properties of the axion field were computed by #cite(<Derevianko:2016vpm>, form: "prose") and #cite(<Foster:2017hbq>, form: "prose"). Implications for the design and interpretation of experiments were discussed by them, and by #cite(<Roberts:2017hla>, form: "prose")@Savalle:2019jsb@Centers:2019dyn@Hui:2020hbq#cite(<Foster:2020fln>). The discussion here follows that in #cite(<Hui:2020hbq>).

A good place to start is to remind ourselves of the relation between the axion $phi.alt$ and the wavefunction $psi$: $ phi.alt ( t , arrow(x) ) = 1 / sqrt(2 m) (psi ( t , arrow(x) ) e^(- i m t) + psi^(*) ( t , arrow(x) ) e^(i m t)) thin . $<phipsiB> Axion detection experiments measure $phi.alt$ or its derivatives via its coupling to photons ($cal(L) tilde.op g_gamma phi.alt F tilde(F)$) and fermions such as quarks or leptons ($cal(L) tilde.op g_Psi partial_mu phi.alt macron(Psi) gamma^mu gamma_5 Psi$).#footnote[The coupling constants $g_gamma$ and $g_Psi$ play the role of $1 / f$ in Equation (#ref(<phiInteractions>, supplement: none)). There is also the coupling to gluons, related to an oscillating electric dipole moment for nucleons #cite(<CASPEr>).] Writing $phi.alt$ in terms of $psi$ reminds us there are two time scales of interest: one is the fast Compton time scale $tilde.op m^(- 1)$ of $phi.alt$ oscillations; the other is the slow de Broglie time scale $tilde.op ( m v^2 )^(- 1)$ of $psi$ fluctuations due to wave interference ($v$ is the velocity dispersion of dark matter; see discussion around @tcohere):

$  &  & t_(o s c .) equiv frac(2 pi, m) = 1.3 thin y r . (frac(10^(- 22) thin upright("eV"), m)) = 4.1 times 10^(- 9) thin upright("s") (frac(10^(- 6) thin upright("eV"), m)) thin ,\
 &  & t_(upright("dB")) equiv frac(2 pi, m v^2) = 1.9 times 10^6 thin y r . (frac(10^(- 22) thin upright("eV"), m)) (frac(250 thin upright("km") / upright("s"), v))^2 thin\
 &  & quad quad = 5.9 times 10^(- 3) thin upright("s") (frac(10^(- 6) thin upright("eV"), m)) (frac(250 thin upright("km") / upright("s"), v))^2 thin . $

